"executive_summary": "This study presents a solvent-free mechanochemical method for synthesizing eco-friendly potassium calcium phosphate fertilizers from eggshell waste (calcite) and monobasic potassium dihydrogen phosphate (KH2PO4). The process utilizes high-energy ball milling to promote solid-state reactions, resulting in the formation of K3CaH(PO4)2 and potentially CaKPO4 phases. The primary objective is to create a 'smart release' fertilizer that improves nutrient management by delaying the bioavailability of phosphorus (P) and potassium (K) while enhancing the bioavailability of calcium (Ca) in acidic soils. Experimental results demonstrate that milling for 2 hours at 600 rpm yields optimal phase formation. In citric acid solution, the milled samples exhibited slower release kinetics for P and K compared to the KH2PO4 precursor, fitting pseudo-second-order models. Conversely, calcium release was significantly faster than from raw eggshell. Soil release assays confirmed these trends, with the milled sample showing a progressive P release (45 mg/kg at 30 days) compared to the rapid release of KH2PO4 (107 mg/kg at 3 days). Furthermore, the formation of hydroxyapatite in soil conditions was identified as a mechanism for long-term P and Ca retention. The P radial diffusion of the milled sample was intermediate, 34% faster than struvite but 22% slower than KH2PO4. This approach leverages reverse logistics to convert food industry waste into a valuable agricultural input, offering a sustainable alternative to conventional fertilizers with improved nutrient efficiency and reduced environmental impact.",
